(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-musculature-quiz"]={"title":"The Axial Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Into what four groups can the axial muscles be placed, based on location or function?","choices":["Muscles of the head and neck, muscles of the vertebral column, muscles of the rib cage/abdominal-pelvic walls, and muscles of the pelvic floor","Superficial muscles, intermediate muscles, deep muscles, and intrinsic muscles, classified purely by their depth within the trunk and back","Muscles that produce flexion, muscles that produce extension, muscles that produce abduction, and muscles that produce adduction at each joint","Muscles of the upper limb, muscles of the lower limb, muscles of the trunk wall, and muscles of the head region alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The axial muscles can be placed into four groups based on location or function: muscles of the head and neck, muscles of the vertebral column, muscles of the rib cage and lateral walls of the abdominal/pelvic cavities, and muscles of the pelvic floor. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates all the muscles of facial expression?","choices":["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"],"answer":1,"exp":"All muscles of facial expression are innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle compresses and purses the lips?","choices":["Mentalis muscle","Risorius muscle","Orbicularis oris","Buccinator muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Orbicularis oris compresses and purses the lips. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle compresses the cheeks, functioning in actions like blowing air or whistling?","choices":["Buccinator","Zygomaticus major","Orbicularis oris","Depressor anguli oris"],"answer":0,"exp":"Buccinator compresses the cheeks. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle elevates the corner of the mouth and draws it laterally, and also elevates the upper lip depending on which portion is described?","choices":["Risorius muscle by itself","Zygomaticus major and minor","Mentalis muscle by itself","Depressor labii inferioris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Zygomaticus major and minor elevate the corner of the mouth and draw it laterally, with zygomaticus minor also elevating the upper lip. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle?","choices":["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral rectus is innervated by the abducens nerve (CN VI), per the mnemonic \"LR6 SO4 3\" (lateral rectus by CN 6, superior oblique by CN 4, and the rest by CN 3). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the superior oblique muscle?","choices":["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Optic nerve (CN II)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (CN IV), per the \"LR6 SO4 3\" mnemonic. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle rotates the eye downward and laterally?","choices":["Inferior oblique","Superior oblique","Medial rectus","Superior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Superior oblique rotates the eye downward and laterally. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What condition results from dysfunction of the levator palpebrae superioris?","choices":["Strabismus (misaligned eyes)","Diplopia limited to lateral gaze only","Nystagmus (involuntary eye movement)","Ptosis (drooping of the upper eyelid)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Dysfunction of the levator palpebrae superioris, which elevates the upper eyelid, causes ptosis. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which muscle furrows the brow in the \"glabella\" area, pulling skin inferiorly and medially to wrinkle the brow?","choices":["Procerus","Levator palpebrae superioris","Corrugator supercilii","Orbicularis oculi"],"answer":2,"exp":"Corrugator supercilii furrows the brow in the glabella area, pulling skin inferiorly and medially to wrinkle the brow, innervated by CN VII. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication opens the jaw, unlike the other muscles in this group which close it?","choices":["Temporalis muscle","Lateral pterygoid","Masseter muscle","Medial pterygoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lateral pterygoid opens the jaw, distinguishing it from the masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid, which act to close the jaw. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What nerve innervates all the muscles of mastication?","choices":["Facial nerve, cranial nerve seven (CN VII)","Accessory nerve, cranial nerve eleven (CN XI)","Mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve twelve (CN XII)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The muscles of mastication are innervated by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (CN V). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Where does the temporalis insert, after passing medial to the zygomatic arch?","choices":["The mental protuberance","The angle and lateral surface of the mandible","The medial surface of the mandibular ramus","The coronoid process of the mandible"],"answer":3,"exp":"The temporalis passes medial to the zygomatic arch to insert on the coronoid process of the mandible. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle depresses the tongue and pulls it inward, and what nerve innervates it?","choices":["Palatoglossus; innervated by CN XII","Hyoglossus; innervated by CN XII","Genioglossus; innervated by CN X","Styloglossus; innervated by CN X"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hyoglossus moves the tongue in and down, and is innervated by CN XII (hypoglossal nerve), like genioglossus and styloglossus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle is the exception to the rule that tongue muscles are innervated by CN XII, instead being innervated by CN X?","choices":["Palatoglossus","Genioglossus","Styloglossus","Hyoglossus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Palatoglossus is innervated by CN X (vagus nerve), unlike genioglossus, hyoglossus, and styloglossus, which are all innervated by CN XII (hypoglossal nerve). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle moves the tongue outward and downward?","choices":["Genioglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus","Palatoglossus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Genioglossus moves the tongue out and down. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which anterior neck muscle, when acting together bilaterally, flexes the neck, and when acting unilaterally, turns the head and face to the opposite side?","choices":["Sternocleidomastoid","Thyrohyoid muscle","Sternothyroid muscle","Omohyoid muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid, innervated by CN XI (accessory nerve), flexes the neck when both sides act together, and turns the head/face to the opposite side when acting alone on one side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the sternocleidomastoid?","choices":["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Accessory nerve (CN XI)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid is innervated by the accessory nerve (CN XI). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which anterior neck muscle depresses the hyoid bone and elevates the larynx?","choices":["Sternohyoid","Digastric","Sternocleidomastoid","Thyrohyoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thyrohyoid depresses the hyoid bone and elevates the larynx. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the pharyngeal constrictor muscles (superior, middle, and inferior)?","choices":["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pharyngeal constrictors (superior, middle, and inferior) are innervated by CN X (vagus nerve). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which group of pharyngeal muscles is innervated by both CN IX and CN X?","choices":["Pharyngeal constrictors (superior, middle, inferior)","Muscles of mastication (temporalis, masseter, pterygoids)","Palatal muscles (tensor veli palatini, levator veli palatini)","Laryngeal elevators (palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus, stylopharyngeus)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The laryngeal elevators — palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus, and stylopharyngeus — are innervated by both CN IX and CN X. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which group of pharyngeal/palatal muscles is innervated by both CN V and CN X?","choices":["Extra-ocular muscles (superior, inferior, medial, lateral rectus)","Laryngeal elevators (palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus)","Palatal muscles (tensor veli palatini, levator veli palatini)","Pharyngeal constrictors (superior, middle, inferior)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The palatal muscles — tensor veli palatini and levator veli palatini — are innervated by CN V and CN X. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What connective tissue layer surrounds the entire muscle?","choices":["Perimysium","Endomysium","External lamina","Epimysium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The epimysium surrounds the entire muscle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What connective tissue layer surrounds bundles of muscle fibers, known as fascicles?","choices":["Endomysium","Sarcolemma","Perimysium","Epimysium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perimysium surrounds bundles of muscle fibers, which are called fascicles. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the sarcolemma?","choices":["A bundle of muscle fibers","The muscle cell membrane","The connective tissue surrounding the entire muscle","The connective tissue surrounding individual muscle fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sarcolemma is the muscle cell membrane. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["The connective tissue sheath, known as the perimysium, that surrounds an individual muscle fascicle","A specialized synapse where a motor neuron communicates with a skeletal muscle fiber to initiate contraction","The fibrous point of attachment where a tendon anchors skeletal muscle firmly onto the underlying bone","A structural junction that mechanically connects two adjacent skeletal muscles to one another"],"answer":1,"exp":"The neuromuscular junction is a specialized synapse where a motor neuron communicates with a skeletal muscle fiber to initiate muscle contraction. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter is contained in the synaptic vesicles of the motor neuron terminal at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Acetylcholine (ACh)","Dopamine transmitter","Norepinephrine (NE)","Glutamate transmitter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The synaptic vesicles at the motor neuron terminal contain the neurotransmitter acetylcholine (ACh). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What enzyme in the synaptic cleft breaks down acetylcholine to terminate the signal at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","Acetylcholine transferase","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE)","Creatine kinase enzyme"],"answer":2,"exp":"Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), found in the synaptic cleft, breaks down ACh to terminate the signal. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the motor end plate, and what receptors does it contain?","choices":["A region of the spinal cord that contains the motor neuron cell bodies themselves","The presynaptic terminal of the motor neuron, containing synaptic vesicles filled with acetylcholine","A specialized region of the sarcolemma containing nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChRs)","The connective tissue sheath surrounding and protecting the entire neuromuscular junction"],"answer":2,"exp":"The motor end plate is a specialized region of the sarcolemma (postsynaptic membrane) that contains nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChRs), which bind ACh and initiate an end-plate potential, and has junctional folds to increase surface area and receptor density. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which supporting cells cap the neuromuscular junction and help maintain its structure and function?","choices":["Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Astrocytes","Microglia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Schwann cells, described as non-myelinating glial cells at this location, cap the neuromuscular junction and help maintain its structure and function. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
